  "worked_solution": "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +6=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-2 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda =2\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation.",
